<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dae Jung-sang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 698</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Qiqi Zhongxiang, Geolgeol Jung-sang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dae Jung-sang was a leader of Goguryeo refugees in the Yingzhou region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>, founder of Balhae. When the Khitan rose against the Tang in 696, he and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geolsa-biu">Geolsa Biu</a> led their followers east towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. The Tang offered him a title, but he died soon afterwards and his son led the people on to found the new state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of his origins. He is also called Kŏlgŏl Chungsang, and one theory holds that he and his son Dae Jo-yeong were in fact the same man, who took a more Sinitic name on becoming king, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> mistook them for father and son. Another theory makes him a Khitan, because the Wudai Huiyao gives him the title Da Sheli. Song Ki-ho notes that the title is not known among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> in the 7th century, while Mark E. Byington suggests that Sheli was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> community north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the fall of Goguryeo his people settled in Yingzhou, modern Chaoyang. In 696 the Khitan leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jinzhong">Li Jinzhong</a> and his brother-in-law Sun Wanrong rose against the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>'s Zhou dynasty, killed an abusive commander and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, and the people of Yingzhou fled east towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a>. The Tang tried to win over the two leaders of the refugees with titles, making him Duke of Zhen and Kŏlsa Piu Duke of Xu. Kŏlsa Piu refused and was soon defeated by a Tang force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kaigu">Li Kaigu</a>, while he fled with his followers.</p><p>Other accounts differ. According to Crossley there was no rebellion at all, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> leader helped the Tang suppress the Khitan rebels. According to Wang Zhenping, three Mohe chieftains used the Khitan rising to move further east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died at about the time of Kŏlsa Piu's defeat, and his son led the group from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> valley to Mount Tianmen, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a>. There, according to the Old Book of Tang, the son united the Goguryeo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe peoples</a> and heavily defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kaigu">Li Kaigu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tianmenling">Battle of Tianmenling</a>, then built a walled settlement against Mount Dongmo and in 698 declared himself King of Zhen. Wang Zhenping calls the son a foster son. Although most of the credit for founding Balhae has gone to the son, many historians still regard the father as the main supporter and leader of the founding. He had another son, Tae Yabal, from whom one of the two lineages of the Balhae royal house descended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tae Chungsang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Khitan chief Li Jinzhong rebelled against Tang in 696, the Goguryeo leader <b>Dae Jung-sang</b> and the Mohe chief Geolsa Biu led their followers east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
